Subject: [PATCH 09484/11362] The Error Lifecycle: name the principle the other
 rules are instances of

Operator directive 2026-08-21. Recorded as FOUNDATIONAL and placed before the
Claude Code Guidelines, because it explains what the rest of the file is for.

This file has accreted roughly thirty MANDATORY disciplines, nearly all written
after an incident. They are not thirty unrelated rules; they are stages of one
six-step cycle -- expect, identify, own, address, correct, prevent -- applied to
different failures. The new section maps each step to the machinery that already
implements it, so a reader meets the principle before the instances instead of
inferring it from thirty examples.

The step worth singling out is the sixth. A lesson recorded as prose costs every
future agent a read and a memory; a lesson converted into a check costs nothing
and cannot be forgotten. So the section requires an explicit answer at incident
close: check, or prose, and why. It also says plainly that prose is sometimes
the right answer -- a check that fires on legitimate work trains people to
bypass it, which is worse than the gap it closes. Both answers are acceptable;
silence is not.

The scientific-method framing in the directive is load-bearing rather than
decorative. A claim that cannot be wrong teaches nothing, which is why this
project holds artifacts to falsifiable gates, prefers a measured negative to an
unmeasured positive, and treats a correction as worth more than a confirmation.

Committed alone: a first attempt swept in another agent's in-flight experiment
files that were mid-edit and unformatted, and ruff correctly refused. Staging is
shared state between concurrent agents here, so stage narrowly and commit fast.

